      },
      "text": "| Form | Lines | Rhyme Scheme | Meter | Key Feature | Example |\n|------|-------|-------------|-------|-------------|---------|\n| Italian Sonnet (Petrarchan) | 14 | ABBAABBA CDECDE (varied) | Iambic pentameter | Volta (turn) between octave and sestet; octave poses problem, sestet resolves | Petrarch, Donne, Hopkins |\n| Shakespearean Sonnet | 14 | ABAB CDCD EFEF GG | Iambic pentameter | Three quatrains develop argument; couplet provides turn/resolution | Shakespeare's 154 sonnets |\n| Spenserian Sonnet | 14 | ABAB BCBC CDCD EE | Iambic pentameter | Interlocking rhyme chains the stanzas | Spenser's Amoretti |\n| Villanelle | 19 | ABA ABA ABA ABA ABA ABAA | Iambic pentameter (flexible) | Two refrains (A1 = line 1, A2 = line 3) alternate as tercet closing lines; both close the final quatrain | \"Do Not Go Gentle\" (Thomas), \"One Art\" (Bishop) |\n| Sestina | 39 | No rhyme; end-word rotation | Flexible | 6 sestets + 1 tercet (envoi); 6 end-words rotate through pattern across stanzas | Arnaut Daniel, Swinburne, Ashbery |\n| Terza rima | Continuous | ABA BCB CDC... | Iambic pentameter | Interlocking tercets; Dante's form for the Divine Comedy | Dante, Shelley's \"Ode to the West Wind\" |\n| Ottava rima | 8-line stanzas | ABABABCC | Iambic pentameter | Italian epic stanza; mock-heroic in English | Ariosto, Byron's Don Juan |\n| Spenserian stanza | 9 lines | ABABBCBCC | First 8: iambic pentameter; final line: alexandrine (6 feet) | Used for extended narrative/meditation | Spenser's Faerie Queene, Keats's \"Eve of St. Agnes\" |"
    },
